   "etapa": "explicar",
   "piezas": [
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "From an order to a favour",
     "texto": [
      "There are many ways to ask for something. The longer and softer the sentence, the more **polite** it sounds:\n\n- **Pásame** la sal. (direct order)\n- **¿Me pasas** la sal?\n- **¿Puedes** pasarme la sal?\n- **¿Podrías** pasarme la sal?\n- **¿Te importaría** pasarme la sal?\n\nTo ask for a favour: **¿Me haces un favor?**, **Tengo que pedirte un favor** or **Necesito que me ayudes**. You can give a reason with **es que**: *¿Me dejas tu coche? Es que el mío está en el taller.*\n\nTo accept: **claro que sí**, **de acuerdo**. To decline tactfully: **me encantaría, pero no puedo**. **No pienso hacerlo** is a very harsh no."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "gramatica",
     "titulo": "The softer, the more polite",
     "texto": [
      "Spanish softens a request by changing the verb:\n\n- **Present**: *¿Me prestas tu maleta?* With friends and family.\n- **Poder in the present**: *¿Puedes prestarme tu maleta?*\n- **Poder in the conditional**: *¿Podrías prestarme tu maleta?* The conditional (**-ía**) sounds less certain, and that is why it is more polite.\n- **Importar**: *¿Te importaría prestarme tu maleta?* It asks whether it bothers the other person: it is the kindest form.\n\nAfter **necesito que** and **quiero que**, the verb goes in the **subjunctive**: *necesito que me ayudes*.\n\nTo decline tactfully: say thank you, say **me encantaría, pero…** and give the reason with **es que**."
     ],
